WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.971
Validation helps reduce common JavaScript security issues, but it must be paired with safe APIs and context-specific controls.

00:00:08.971 --> 00:00:17.022
XSS risk appears when untrusted data reaches an output context without appropriate encoding or safe rendering.

00:00:17.022 --> 00:00:26.914
Validation can reject data that does not fit a field's expectations, but output encoding and safe templating are still required.

00:00:26.914 --> 00:00:35.425
Injection risk appears when data is interpreted as part of a query, command, expression, template, or protocol.

00:00:35.425 --> 00:00:45.776
A validation rule may narrow the accepted input, but parameterized queries, safe command invocation, and structured APIs are the primary protections.

00:00:45.776 --> 00:00:53.367
Avoid building sensitive operations by concatenating untrusted strings, even when the strings passed basic validation.

00:00:53.367 --> 00:01:01.879
Prototype pollution, at a high level, involves unsafe object handling where input can influence object prototype behavior.

00:01:01.879 --> 00:01:09.010
Validation can help by rejecting unexpected keys, limiting nested structures, and controlling merge behavior.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:20.281
Mass assignment is related: if request data is copied directly into objects or database records, users may set properties they should not control.

00:01:20.281 --> 00:01:21.431
Node.

00:01:21.431 --> 00:01:29.943
js applications also need to consider path traversal, command injection, unsafe deserialization patterns, and insecure file uploads.

00:01:29.943 --> 00:01:37.534
Validation should normalize paths, constrain filenames, check file metadata, limit sizes, and enforce allow-listed fields.

00:01:37.534 --> 00:01:40.984
Still, validation is only one layer.

00:01:40.984 --> 00:01:47.655
Safe filesystem APIs, authorization, sandboxing where appropriate, and careful output handling remain necessary.
